---
import CourseLesson from "../../../layouts/CourseLesson.astro";
import Math from "../../../components/course/Math.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/course/Callout.astro";
import Quiz from "../../../components/course/Quiz.astro";
import ProbabilityTree from "../../../components/course/widgets/ProbabilityTree.astro";
---

<CourseLesson course="probability" lessonSlug="total-probability-and-trees">
  <h2>Partition the world</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>partition</strong> of <Math tex="S" /> is a set of events{" "}
    <Math tex="A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n" /> that are pairwise disjoint and together cover everything.
    Exactly one of them happens, always. The simplest partition is{" "}
    <Math tex="\{A, A^c\}" />.
  </p>
  <p>
    Given a partition, any event <Math tex="B" /> is chopped into the slices{" "}
    <Math tex="B \cap A_i" />, which are disjoint. Axiom 3 lets you add them, and the multiplication
    rule rewrites each slice as a product:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="P(B) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} P(B \cap A_i) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} P(B \mid A_i)\,P(A_i)" />
  <p>
    That is the <strong>law of total probability</strong>: a weighted average of conditional
    probabilities, with the weights being how likely each condition is.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="intuition" label="How to read a tree diagram">
    <p>
      Each level of the tree is one stage of the experiment. The branches out of any node are a
      partition, so their labels sum to 1. <strong>Multiply along a path</strong> to get the
      probability of that complete story; <strong>add across paths</strong> that end in the outcome
      you care about. That is literally the formula above, drawn.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Worked example: the coin-and-die game</h2>
  <p>
    A fair coin is flipped and the player calls it. If the call is correct the player rolls a
    5-sided die; otherwise a 6-sided die. What is the probability of rolling an even number?
  </p>
  <p>
    Stage one partitions on the call: <Math tex="P(\text{right}) = P(\text{wrong}) = \tfrac12" />.
    Stage two is the die. A d5 shows <Math tex="\{1,2,3,4,5\}" />, of which two are even, so{" "}
    <Math tex="P(\text{even} \mid \text{d5}) = \tfrac25" />. A d6 has three of six even, so{" "}
    <Math tex="P(\text{even} \mid \text{d6}) = \tfrac12" />.
  </p>
  <Math
    display
    tex="P(\text{even}) = \tfrac12 \cdot \tfrac25 + \tfrac12 \cdot \tfrac12 = \tfrac15 + \tfrac14 = \tfrac{9}{20} = 0.45"
  />

  <h3>Now bias the coin</h3>
  <p>
    Suppose the coin lands heads with probability <Math tex="p \ne \tfrac12" /> and you know it. You
    would naturally call the likelier side, making your chance of calling correctly{" "}
    <Math tex="q = \max(p, 1-p) > \tfrac12" />. Redo the average with a general{" "}
    <Math tex="q" />:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="P(\text{even}) = q \cdot \tfrac25 + (1-q) \cdot \tfrac12 = \tfrac12 - \tfrac{q}{10}" />
  <p>
    The coefficient on <Math tex="q" /> is <em>negative</em>. Calling the flip correctly wins you the
    d5, and the d5 is the <em>worse</em> die for evens &mdash; only 2 of 5 faces instead of 3 of 6.
    So a known bias does help, but only if you deliberately call the flip <strong>wrong</strong>,
    driving <Math tex="q \to 0" /> and <Math tex="P(\text{even}) \to \tfrac12" />.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="intuition" label="The general lesson">
    <p>
      In a weighted average, shifting weight only helps if you shift it toward the larger
      conditional probability. Always compare <Math tex="P(B \mid A_1)" /> against{" "}
      <Math tex="P(B \mid A_2)" /> before deciding whether influencing stage one is worth anything.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Drag the die sizes below and watch the verdict flip: with a d6 reward and a d5 penalty, calling
    correctly suddenly becomes the thing you want.
  </p>

  <ProbabilityTree />

  <h2>Bayes' rule falls out for free</h2>
  <p>
    The multiplication rule can be written two ways, and setting them equal reverses the
    conditioning:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="P(A_i \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A_i)\,P(A_i)}{P(B)} = \frac{P(B \mid A_i)\,P(A_i)}{\sum_j P(B \mid A_j)\,P(A_j)}" />
  <p>
    The denominator is just the law of total probability again. So "what is the chance the roll was
    even?" and "given that the roll was even, what is the chance the call was correct?" use the same
    three numbers. For the fair-coin game:{" "}
    <Math tex="P(\text{right} \mid \text{even}) = \frac{0.5 \times 0.4}{0.45} = \tfrac{4}{9}" />.
  </p>

  <Quiz
    question="A box holds 2 fair coins and 1 two-headed coin. You draw one at random and flip it. What is P(heads)?"
    options={["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"]}
    correctIndex={1}
    correctText="Correct: (2/3)(1/2) + (1/3)(1) = 1/3 + 1/3 = 2/3. Partition on which coin was drawn, then average."
    incorrectText="Partition on the coin: with probability 2/3 it is fair (heads with probability 1/2), with probability 1/3 it is two-headed (heads for certain). That gives (2/3)(1/2) + (1/3)(1) = 2/3."
  />
</CourseLesson>
